\title{P8 - The gap to the leaders on Mathlib, declaration by declaration}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{reference-engine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P8.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Profiler P8, 2026-11-24 (15:30Z - 17:00Z). No production code was changed. Scratch, tools and raw data: \code{\verb~|work/prof/P8/w9/~}. Living summary: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-P8.md~}. Board lines: \code{\verb~|work/status/requests.log~} (16:20Z, 16:35Z, later ones).}

\p{Question: the machine checks Mathlib at about 4 times gruszka's CPU time and Init at about 2.7 times. Why is Mathlib worse, and what do the leaders do on the declarations where we lose most?}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{In instructions the machine is 6.5 times gruszka on Mathlib and 4.7 times on Init} (per unit, inside \code{\verb~|Session.check~}, joined by name over 714,712 units: 2,438.6 G against 373.8 G; nano-lean 319.3 G: 7.6 times). The CPU-time ratio looks smaller only because the machine retires about twice as many instructions per second as gruszka (13.6 G/s against 6.1 G/s; both on CPUs 18 and 19 in the arena container, gruszka's per-declaration profile by the baseline team the night before).}
\li{\strong{The whole Mathlib-specific part is one class of work: conversion of proofs.} 48 \% of the machine's instructions on Mathlib are in 142,000 units (20 \% of the units) in which the machine needs 8 to 200 times gruszka's instructions (18 times as a class). The other 80 \% of the units are at 4.1 times, the Init ratio. In those 142,000 units the machine runs 10 to 93 definition unfoldings per unfolding of gruszka; over the whole corpus inference is at parity (INFER + CHECK events are 0.98 of gruszka's infer misses).}
\li{\strong{Mechanism}: two instance paths that meet (\code{\verb~|Semiring.toAddCommMonoid ..~} against \code{\verb~|AddCommGroup.toAddCommMonoid ..~}) are compared field by field; every proof field, and every proof argument of two applications of one constant, goes through proof irrelevance, which, as in the official kernel, compares the TYPES of the two proofs: the statements of \code{\verb~|add_comm~}, \code{\verb~|mul_zero~}, \code{\verb~|left_distrib~}, ..., in which every \code{\verb~|+~}, \code{\verb~|*~} and \code{\verb~|0~} is unfolded again on both sides. gruszka and nano-lean never compare a proof argument or a proof field, and they try the arguments of two applications of one head before unfolding it.}
\li{\p{\strong{The machine already contains both shortcuts and keeps them off on purpose} (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~}, NOTES-M4 D27): the soundness review showed hostile exports on which they give a verdict the official checker does not give (S-F1 / S-F2), and owner note 11 says "resemble official by default". Measured on all of Mathlib (every unit accepted in every run):}
\table{
\tr{\th{switches (native -O3, 1 worker, machine jar a5313e0b6c32)}\th{ticks}\th{instructions in checks}\th{against gruszka}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} (the published step-1 set)}\td{1,511.5 M}\td{2,438.6 G}\td{6.52}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,facts~} (official-exact)}\td{1,436.1 M}\td{2,410.5 G}\td{6.45}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,facts,probes~} (official-exact)}\td{1,449.8 M}\td{2,435.1 G}\td{6.51}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,probes,code,casetrees,lazyeval,k2~} (the rich gated set)}\td{1,367.7 M}\td{2,212.5 G}\td{5.92}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,facts~} + \code{\verb~|skipargs~} (NOT official-exact)}\td{876.8 M}\td{1,673.7 G (-31.4 \%)}\td{4.48}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,facts,probes~} + \code{\verb~|congruence~} (NOT official-exact)}\td{807.6 M}\td{1,640.5 G (-32.7 \%)}\td{4.39}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,facts,probes~} + \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~} (NOT official-exact)}\td{680.1 M}\td{\strong{1,450.5 G (-40.5 \%)}}\td{3.88}}
}
\p{The two options overlap: each alone removes about a third, because they answer the same comparisons from two sides (\code{\verb~|skipargs~} does not pose them; \code{\verb~|congruence~} answers them by the same-head probe and from the slots of the two frames instead of unfolding).}
\p{With them the machine is at 3.9 times gruszka on Mathlib, every class of unit between 3.3 and 5.5, every large library area between 2.2 and 6.2, and the units at 8 times and more carry 11.8 \% of its instructions instead of 48.3 \%. On a quiet host (Mathlib slice, section 4.7) the checks take 27 \% less CPU time. On Init, Std and cedar the same options are worth 3.6 \%, 5.0 \% and 6.0 \%: the mechanism is specific to Mathlib's algebraic hierarchy and category theory.}}
\li{\strong{So the answer to "why is Mathlib worse" is a policy, not a defect}: 40 \% of the machine's Mathlib time is the price of comparing proofs exactly as the official kernel does. Nothing that is official-exact and switched off today recovers it (\code{\verb~|facts~}, \code{\verb~|probes~}: 0.1 \% to 1.2 \% of the instructions; the rich gated set 9 \%). Either the owner accepts verdicts that are sound by the theory but not the official algorithm's on hostile exports (both leaders have the same shortcuts in their sources; I did not run them on the hostile exports), or an official-exact way to answer these comparisons cheaply has to be found (section 4.1 says what the trace suggests and what I could not establish).}
\li{\strong{Second finding: the eager-reduction decline rule (M6-F26) costs 11 to 12 \% of the worker's CPU time on Mathlib.} 6,711 units (0.94 \%) are declined and checked by the reference engine: 23.5 s of thread CPU against 177.7 s for the 708,001 units the machine accepts. For gruszka they are 6.3 \% of the corpus (3.5 s).}
\li{\strong{What remains after both is uniform}: 1.5 times gruszka's ticks, 1.3 times its values, 2.6 times its instructions per tick, in every class. That part is not about what is computed but about what one operation costs (profilers P1, P5, P6).}
}

\p{Numbers of this page are instruction and event counts unless a unit of time is given; the CPU seconds are thread CPU clocks of one container on CPUs 18 and 19 of a loaded host and are NOT quiet-host measurements; the one quiet-host measurement is in section 4.7.}
}

