\tr{\td{nano-lean, per unit}\td{instructions: \code{\verb~|work/baselines/mathlib-profile.nano-lean.j1.tsv.gz~}; its 58 counters: \code{\verb~|work/workloads/dyn/nl/*.stats.tsv~} (research 13)}}
\tr{\td{traces}\td{a private copy of the machine's sources of 16:05Z with two hooks (DELTA and CONV events with the judgments on the host stack; \code{\verb~|work/prof/P8/w9/mtrace~}), JVM, on the Mathlib slice \code{\verb~|Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.cone~}}}
}

\p{The join is by declaration name. The machine's units are the definitions, theorems, opaque constants and axioms (714,712 on Mathlib); inductive types, constructors and recursors are not checked by an engine. 99.1 \% of gruszka's and 99.3 \% of nano-lean's per-declaration instructions are in the joined units.}

\p{The 248 s / 68 s of the question were measured on machine jar f82074b207f1 (13:4xZ), before the eager-reduction rule: there the machine accepted all 714,712 units with 1,583 M ticks and 3,031 G instructions for the whole process. The jar measured here declines 6,711 units and has 1,511.5 M ticks.}
}

