\subtree{
\title{4. Mechanisms}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 Proofs are compared by their types (the official rule); the leaders do not compare them at all}

\p{\strong{Size: 31.4 \% of the machine's Mathlib instructions (42 \% of its ticks) for the proof positions alone; 40.5 \% (55 \%) together with 4.2; the two overlap (4.2 alone: 32.7 \%).} Owner of the place: M4 (\code{\verb~|Conv.java~}, \code{\verb~|Cargs.java~}), facts by M5 (\code{\verb~|Facts.propMask~}); the rule that keeps it off: NOTES-M4 D27, findings S-F1 / S-F2 of the soundness review, owner note 11.}

\p{Trace (\code{\verb~|out/trace-scheme-s1.txt~}; unit \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry.Spec.sheafedSpaceMap_hom_c_app~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{ticks / steps}\th{unfoldings}\th{conversion problems}\th{inference}}
\tr{\td{machine \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}}\td{65,363}\td{5,359 DELTA}\td{2,227 CONV + 1,868 pair hits, 2,086 PIRR, 3,383 VTYPE}\td{132 INFER, 5 CHECK}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts~}}\td{62,106}\td{5,359}\td{892 PIRR, 2,001 VTYPE}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts~} + \code{\verb~|skipargs~}}\td{8,091}\td{589}\td{298 CONV, 29 PIRR}\td{130 INFER}}
\tr{\td{machine + \code{\verb~|facts,probes~} + \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~}}\td{3,849}\td{221}\td{187 CONV, 10 PIRR}\td{}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{222}\td{10}\td{25 def_eq, 14 cache hits}\td{33 infer misses}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{162}\td{9}\td{82 def_eq, 76 pointer-equal}\td{21 infer misses}}
}

\p{What happens (events 15 to 50 of the trace): while the type of the theorem is inferred, \code{\verb~|Module R R inst ..~} is compared with \code{\verb~|Module R R inst' ..~}; the instance arguments are \code{\verb~|Semiring.toAddCommMonoid R (CommSemiring.toSemiring ..)~} and \code{\verb~|AddCommGroup.toAddCommMonoid R (Ring.toAddCommGroup ..)~}. Lazy delta unfolds both, structure eta (ETAS) compares the fields; field 1 is \code{\verb~|add_comm~}, a proof: CONV, not identical, PIRR, and PIRR, as \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_proof_irrel~}, infers both types and compares them: \code{\verb~|(a b : R) -> a + b = b + a~} with \code{\verb~|+~} through one path against the same with \code{\verb~|+~} through the other. \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, \code{\verb~|instHAdd~}, \code{\verb~|Add.add~}, \code{\verb~|AddMonoid.toAdd~} are projection functions (reducibility hint abbrev), so the official order does not try their arguments first: both sides are unfolded down to the structure. That repeats for every occurrence of an operation in every law of every structure on the path. The 5,359 DELTA events of this unit: 610 \code{\verb~|Mul.mul~}, 590 \code{\verb~|HMul.hMul~}, 360 \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, 360 \code{\verb~|Add.add~}, 172 \code{\verb~|OfNat.ofNat~}, 147 \code{\verb~|Membership.mem~}, 104 \code{\verb~|AddCommMonoid.toAddMonoid~}, 104 \code{\verb~|instHMul~} ...; the host stacks: \code{\verb~|DELTA<LSTEP<LAZYD<CONV<CARGS<CONV<CBIND<CONV<PIRR<CONV<CARGS~} (910), \code{\verb~|..<CONV<ETAS<..~}. The conversion problems by head: 230 \code{\verb~|HMul.hMul =?= HMul.hMul~}, 209 Pi, 194 lambda, 181 \code{\verb~|Eq~}, 148 \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd~}, 102 \code{\verb~|Exists~}, and the proofs themselves: \code{\verb~|Semiring.mul_zero =?= Semiring.mul_zero~} (18), \code{\verb~|Semiring.left_distrib~} (16) ...}

\p{The extreme cases are instance fields: \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.instAddCommGroupEllAdicCohomology._proof_21~} costs the machine 2,204,336 ticks and 2.91 G instructions, gruszka 144 ticks and 90,700 instructions (32,000 times); with \code{\verb~|skipargs~} 604 ticks, with \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~} 175. Twenty-odd \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} / \code{\verb~|_aux_N~} units of that one instance are 45 G instructions, 1.8 \% of the machine's Mathlib.}

\p{What the leaders do instead:}

\ul{
\li{gruszka, \code{\verb~|refs/gruszka/src/tc.rs~}: \code{\verb~|spines_defeq_skip~} (8573-8600) compares the arguments of two applications of one constant except the positions of \code{\verb~|prop_positions(row, levels)~} (8641: positions whose declared domain is surely a proposition) and, inside lazy delta, of \code{\verb~|unused_args(row)~} (8603: positions the body never reads); \code{\verb~|try_eta_struct~} (8823) skips the proof fields of the constructor the same way; \code{\verb~|surely_not_proof~} (7297) answers "is it a proof" without inference; \code{\verb~|proof_irrel~} (7499) is reached only for what is left.}
\li{nano-lean, \code{\verb~|refs/nano-lean/src/value_checker/conv.rs~}: \code{\verb~|relevant~} (389) is the same mask (unused and Prop-typed arguments), \code{\verb~|probe_args~} (359) tries the arguments of two applications of the same head BEFORE \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} and before proof irrelevance, for every unfoldable head (the official kernel only for \code{\verb~|regular~} hints, inside lazy delta), with a step budget; \code{\verb~|statically_not_proof~} (315).}
\li{The machine has the same three things: the comparison plan of CARGS as a skip mask (B3 proof positions, B4 unread positions), the Prop mask of ETAS (\code{\verb~|Conv.etasFields~}), static PROOF (B7). B7 is in \code{\verb~|facts~} and is official-exact; B3, B4 and the ETAS mask need \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.m4=skipargs~} since D27.}
}

\p{Is it official-exact? \strong{No, and that is documented}: the soundness review (S-F2) and M4 hold exports that the official checker rejects and that these shortcuts accept (the official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not a congruence where a reduction inspects a proof; \code{\verb~|M4OfficialTest~} pins three of them). The equations the shortcuts assume are valid in the theory, so this is "sound by the theory, not the official algorithm's verdict"; both leaders' sources contain the same shortcuts (I did not run the leaders on the hostile exports). It is not a soundness hole of the kind that proves \code{\verb~|False~}, as far as the review and M4's notes say; I did not re-examine that.}

\p{What an official-exact form would have to do, from the trace: the expensive comparisons are of the form T\verb~|[~A\verb~|]~ against T\verb~|[~B\verb~|]~, where T is the same domain term (the statement of a law) and A, B are two instance paths whose comparison is already in the pair table as POS. The official kernel has no better tool for this either (its success cache holds exact pairs only since v4.35, research 02 section 5; it needs 30 times gruszka's instructions on Mathlib, and how much of that is this class I did not measure). Candidates the owners could examine, none of which I verified: (a) when the two types are the same term under two frames, compare by walking the term once with the frames in lockstep and consult the pair table at the slots (\code{\verb~|Same~} of M4, D28, does the walk but answers only for identical slots); the question is whether "slots POS, term identical" implies the official verdict for PROPOSITIONS that contain no proof-inspecting reduction, which is the case M4 could not close in general; (b) the comparison of the two unfolded sides repeats the same unfold-to-structure steps for each occurrence: a memo of "DELTA + CORE of \code{\verb~|HAdd.hAdd A x y~} is \code{\verb~|proj Add 0 (toAdd A) x y~}" per (head, instance arguments) would keep the official steps and remove most of the events - the steps are official, only their cost changes. (b) is engineering inside the official rule; its size is bounded by the 31 \% and I could not measure it without building it. That \code{\verb~|congruence~} ALONE (the proofs' types are still compared, but the same-head probe B1 and the closure congruence B12 answer before anything is unfolded) removes 32.7 \% says that the cost is in how T\verb~|[~A\verb~|]~ against T\verb~|[~B\verb~|]~ is decided, not in the fact that it is asked: candidate (a) is exactly the part of \code{\verb~|congruence~} that would have to be proven exact for propositions.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 Congruence before unfolding}

\p{\strong{Size: 32.7 \% of the machine's Mathlib instructions alone (2,435.1 G -> 1,640.5 G with \code{\verb~|facts,probes~}; ticks 1,449.8 M -> 807.6 M), 9 \% on top of 4.1} (1,673.7 G -> 1,450.5 G). Owner: M4 (\code{\verb~|Conv.congruence~}: B1, B2, B12). Off since D27 for the same reason (S-F1).}

\p{gruszka: \code{\verb~|closure_congr~} (\code{\verb~|tc.rs~} 6792) and \code{\verb~|same_term~} (7026): two closures of the same term and level substitution whose READ environment entries are pairwise convertible are equal; tried after every delta step. nano-lean: \code{\verb~|same(t, s, fuel 512)~} (\code{\verb~|conv.rs~} 107) and the same-head probe. In \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.SpecMap_presheaf_map_eqToHom~} the machine's DELTA events go 16,140 -> 1,564 (\code{\verb~|skipargs~}) -> 137 (\code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~}), and 137 is exactly gruszka's unfold count for that unit; ticks 186,343 -> 21,932 -> 5,243 against gruszka's 4,208. With both options the machine's event counts are within a factor 1.5 of gruszka's in the units I looked at.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 Eager-reduction declines (M6-F26): the reference engine checks Mathlib's heaviest proofs}

\p{\strong{Size: 23.5 s of 210 s of the worker's thread CPU (11 to 12 \%), up to 20 s of it recoverable.} Owner: M6 (\code{\verb~|MachineSession~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.enterER~}), armed by M5 / M7.}

\p{6,711 units enter eager reduction (an argument \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~}); 6,538 are \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} theorems, 6,017 under \code{\verb~|Mathlib.*~}, 400 under \code{\verb~|Batteries.*~}. The machine spends 1.2 s (9.5 M ticks, 15.3 G instructions) before declining them, declines each twice (the driver's cold retry is answered at once), and the reference engine decides them: 66.5 M reference steps, 23.5 s of thread CPU between the checks of those units. For gruszka the same units are 23.9 G instructions = 6.3 \% of its corpus and 3.5 s, for nano-lean 7.6 \%; they contain the most expensive declarations of Mathlib for both leaders (\code{\verb~|..sym2x_add_sub_eq_addSubMap_sym2x._proof_1_8~}: 1.89 G instructions for gruszka; \code{\verb~|IsEllipticNet.atomRel_of_even_odd._proof_1_15~}; \code{\verb~|SzemerediRegularity.edgeDensity_star_not_uniform._proof_1_10~}). On Init 164 units, on Std 203, on cedar 423 are declined the same way. Official-exactness is the reason for the rule (the official answer under \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~} depends on per-declaration caches); the cost was not in any table. With several workers these units are also the longest ones of the run, so the fallback sets the tail of the wall clock (P7's topic; not measured here).}

\p{The other 708,001 units cost 10.5 microseconds of thread CPU each between two checks (7.5 s, 3.5 \% of the worker): the driver's bookkeeping, admission and 40,568 session resets.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 What the official-exact switches give on Mathlib}

\table{
\tr{\th{switch set}\th{ticks}\th{instructions}\th{}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}}\td{1,511.5 M}\td{2,438.6 G}\td{}}
\tr{\td{+ \code{\verb~|facts~} (static PROOF: PIRR 41.5 M events fall, VTYPE with them)}\td{1,436.1 M (-5.0 \%)}\td{2,410.5 G (-1.2 \%)}\td{official-exact}}
\tr{\td{+ \code{\verb~|facts,probes~}}\td{1,449.8 M (-4.1 \%)}\td{2,435.1 G (-0.1 \%)}\td{official-exact}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,probes,code,casetrees,lazyeval,k2~}}\td{1,367.7 M (-9.5 \%)}\td{2,212.5 G (-9.3 \%)}\td{gated by the notes}}
}

\p{None of them touches the class of section 4.1: the traced unit keeps its 5,360 DELTA events under every one of these sets (65,363 / 66,769 / 60,684 / 63,599 ticks under \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, \code{\verb~|+probes~}, the rich set, and \code{\verb~|facts,probes,lazyeval,m4a~}).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.5 Sessions}

\p{On the Mathlib slice the session cap moves the ticks by at most 16 \%: 1 MiB 31.7 M, 4 MiB (default) 26.9 M, 16 MiB 24.6 M, 64 MiB 23.0 M, 1 GiB 22.7 M. A larger warm session is therefore not the Mathlib factor (and costs CPU time through cache misses, as the integrator measured on Init). Units that gruszka answers almost for free because an earlier declaration of the session inferred the same closed term (e.g. \code{\verb~|List.MergeSort.Internal.splitRevInTwo._proof_2~}: 5 ticks for gruszka, 48,358 for the machine) exist in every corpus; after \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~} the units at 64 times and more are 1.3 \% of the machine's Mathlib instructions. That is the upper bound of what shared check facts (\code{\verb~|pchk~}, and its extension to terms with level parameters, which most of Mathlib's are) can still bring; I did not separate it further.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.6 What remains: the cost of one operation}

\p{With \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~} (Mathlib): 680.1 M ticks against gruszka's 454.4 M (1.50), 345.2 M records against 263.9 M values interned (1.31), 2,133 instructions per tick against 823 (2.6), 4,200 instructions per record against 1,416 values. The ratio per class of unit is between 3.3 and 5.5, per library area between 2.2 and 6.2 (the large areas; the largest: the \code{\verb~|Init~} part of the export at 6.2, \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} units of Init that evaluate). This is the same gap that Init shows (4.7 in instructions for the whole process; 90.9 G inside the checks against about 20.5 G for gruszka's checking, i.e. about 4.4), and it is the subject of the function-level profiles (hash-consing probes, PRUNE, frames), not of this report.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.7 CPU time on quiet cores (the one timing measurement of this report)}

\p{\code{\verb~|bench/quiet.py -- work/prof/P8/w9/quiet-slice.sh~} (16:38Z; the 8 reserved performance cores; native -O3, one worker, statistics off; Mathlib slice \code{\verb~|Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.cone~}, 21,127 units; two rounds; thread CPU of the checks summed by the tap, process user time by GNU time):}

\table{
\tr{\th{switches}\th{ticks}\th{thread CPU of the checks, s}\th{process user s}\th{ns per tick}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}}\td{26.95 M}\td{2.709 / 2.631}\td{3.17 / 3.08}\td{99}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|+facts~} and \code{\verb~|skipargs~}}\td{18.58 M (-31 \%)}\td{2.137 / 2.146 (\strong{-20 \%})}\td{2.57 / 2.58}\td{115}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|+facts,probes~} and \code{\verb~|congruence,skipargs~}}\td{15.48 M (-43 \%)}\td{1.912 / 1.974 (\strong{-27 \%})}\td{2.35 / 2.43}\td{125}}
}

\p{The ticks that disappear are cheaper than the average tick (conversion bookkeeping against evaluation and inference), so the CPU saving is about two thirds of the tick saving. On all of Mathlib the tick saving is 55 \% and the instruction saving 40.5 \%; the thread CPU of the checks in the loaded container went from 178.9 s to 108.8 s (-39 \%), which fits, but that pair is not a quiet measurement.}
}
}

