}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What could not be established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{CPU seconds on a quiet host for all of Mathlib.} The Mathlib CPU-time figures of this report (178.9 s for the checks of the plain set, 108.8 s with the two options, 23.5 s of fallback) are thread CPU clocks from single runs in a container on CPUs 18 and 19 while the host was loaded (load average 50 to 140). Counts and instructions are exact; those seconds are directions. The quiet window (section 4.7) covers the Mathlib slice only: the lock had a queue of 13 minutes, and a full Mathlib pair would have held it for 8.}
\li{\strong{The warm wall clock with 8 workers} (owner note 12's first number): everything here is one worker; the effect of findings 1 and 2 on the parallel wall clock is P7's to measure (finding 2 concerns the longest units of the run).}
\li{\strong{Whether an official-exact form of mechanisms 1 and 2 exists}, and how much of the 40 \% it would keep. Section 4.1 names two candidates; neither was built or checked against the hostile exports.}
\li{\strong{gruszka's instructions per unit on cedar and Std} (its instruction profile exists for Mathlib only); the contrast rows use its tick counter.}
\li{\strong{How the 23.5 s of the eager-reduction fallback split} between the reference engine and the resets around it, and its effect on the wall clock with workers.}
\li{\strong{The split of the remaining instructions by judgment} (events are exact, instructions per event were not regressed).}
\li{The private trace build was made from the working tree of 16:05Z, which is later than the measured jar; on the slice it counts 31.6 M ticks where the measured jar counts 26.9 M (the traced units agree to 0.1 \%; the jar gives 26.95 M on the JVM launcher with the same heap, so it is not the launcher). The units that differ are those that follow a heavy unit of the same structure and were answered from the warm session before (\code{\verb~|CommRingCat.limitCommRing._proof_46~}: 1,036 -> 59,894 ticks): the session is lost more often in the later tree. I did not find out which change does it (\code{\verb~|work/prof/P8/w9/tmp/units-trace-scheme.tsv~} against \code{\verb~|data/units-n-s1-scheme.tsv~}; board line of 16:55Z).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Reproduction}

\pre{\verb~|
P=work/prof/P8/w9
# the tap jar and the native image (published jars of 15:36Z + the tap)
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- $P/build-ni.sh $P/dist-tap
# counts on the host (ticks, records, events per op): data/units-TAG-CORPUS.tsv
$P/run-tap.sh native n-s1 scheme warm,gated,expect
# instructions per unit in the container (instrtap): data/instr-TAG-CORPUS.txt
bench/slot.sh -- bench/arena-docker.sh --cpuset-cpus 18,19 --memory 16g --no-network \
    env P8_STATS=1 $P/run-instr.sh t-s1 mathlib warm,gated,expect
bench/slot.sh -- bench/arena-docker.sh --cpuset-cpus 18,19 --memory 16g --no-network $P/batch-mathlib.sh \
    "t-s1fp-cong:warm,gated,expect,facts,probes:-Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs"
# tables
python3 -I $P/scripts/mkcache.py t-s1 mathlib            # joined per-unit table (pickle)
python3 -I $P/scripts/join.py t-s1 mathlib --metric instr --lmetric instr --base 4.7
python3 -I $P/scripts/fam.py t-s1 mathlib
# trace of named units (private machine copy with hooks, JVM)
P9_ONLY=Name1,Name2 P9_OUT=out.txt java -cp "$P/mtrace/classes:$P/snap/lib/*" rlvm.cli.Main -j 1 \
    -X engine=p8tap -X machine.switches=warm,gated,expect EXPORT.ndjson
~}

\p{Files: \code{\verb~|data/units-*.tsv~} (one line per check), \code{\verb~|data/instr-*.txt~}, \code{\verb~|data/gruszka-mathlib.raw~}, \code{\verb~|out/join-*.md~}, \code{\verb~|out/fam-mathlib-s1.md~}, \code{\verb~|out/trace-scheme-s1.txt~}, \code{\verb~|logs/runs.log~} (every run with its switches).}
}
